s the continuity to at least  know that genetic testing will stratify  patients with disease as to whether they  won't benefit from this in certain  circumstances  i realize you can't see this well but  this is meant to indicate  the ability to do testing  of the  egf receptor in  breast cancer  looking at  response to drugs that target that or a  particular  b raft mutation that  determines sensitivity to specific drugs  in melanoma  and now increasingly even whole genome  sequencing is being applied in cancer  therapeutics to make a precise diagnosis  and to select the most appropriate  mechanism of treatment so we have moved  towards a kind of  so-called precision medicine model at  least in some areas especially in cancer  there are also pharmacogenetic  polymorphisms that can be tested  that provide the possibility of  adjusting drug dosage according to the  metabolism of the given individual this  diagram  shows the metabolism of  warfarin very commonly used  anticoagulant  where the v core c1 target of warfarin  is subject to polymorphisms that  determine its sensitivity to warfarin  and the cyp2c9  gene is involved in metabolism of  warfarin and you can use testing of the  two of these to identify the likely  optimal dose of warfarin for a given  patient  it is pretty clearly  clinically valid it is still being  subjected to clinical trials though to  establish clinical utility and  cost-effectiveness  this is an example i think of a kind of  test that is likely to be incorporated  into electronic prescribing systems  whether physicians will ever need to  know in their head how to addre adjust  drug dosage warfarin i'm not so sure but  you can easily imagine it would sit in  the background in a  electronic prescribing system and then  inform the  drug dosage that is chosen  a lot of people have um wondered about  the possibility of  genome sequencing and actually as i look  at the slide i can see some  errors in terms of the translation from  the platform where they were created to  the  display platform here but i don't think  it has any major effect  the question would be should everybody  have their genome sequence and if they  did  when would you do it and where would you  keep the information  it's possible to actually do a prenatal  genome sequence even on the  very small amount of fetal dna that  is released usually from the placenta  into the maternal circulation and is now  already being used for non-invasive  prenatal screening could be used for  even prenatal  genome sequencing  you can imagine doing it instead of the  newborn screen metabolic test that's  currently done  that i've already talked about  either of these raises a lot of  questions about do you want to know  about adult onset disorders prenatally  or in the newborn period  you could do it in childhood you still  have at least the possibility of  intervening but  complexity of knowing about things that  are not immediately relevant to the  health of the child  or you can do it in adulthood when you  can certainly provide informed consent  about the procedure  but if you wait till then you have lost  the opportunity to intervene at an  earlier point  and if it were to be done you could ask  the question where would the information  live  so one possibility shown at the lower  left is in the electronic health record  which probably makes the stewards of  these records and different institutions  a bit nervous to realize the vast  storage requirements that would be  expected of them although probably  storage will become cheaper and cheaper  as time goes on making that a  possibility but another challenge faced  is that most people do not spend their  entire lifetime receiving health care  from a single  place wherever it might be academic or  otherwise people tend to move around  over time or you might be traveling when  some medical event occurs that requires  or at least would be informed by genomic  information so  not clear whether the ehr is 