asserby  trying to come to his aid and i used  that  sort of picture because  probably many of you know that in the  last year the american college of  medical genetics and genomics has  proposed that laboratories that are  doing exome or genome sequencing  query 56 genes for specific types of  mutations an example would be the  brca one and two genes  that those results be returned to the  referring position regardless of whether  the test was done for that reason  that the patients not be asked whether  they do or don't want those lab results  returned  the idea would be that the physician  understands the patient's circumstances  better than the lab does  and can contextualize the results decide  what and how to share with the patient  and provide appropriate support and  counseling in the process it's been a  controversial recommendation  the genes were chosen very carefully i'm  sure we've left some off  that could potentially find their way on  the list and the doubt that the list  will stay just as it is forever but um  the thinking was this is a bit analogous  to finding an unexpected pulmonary  nodule and a chest x-ray that's being  done to  see if somebody fractured a rib  you don't ask do i want to know about  the possibility of incidental  cancer  identification or potential cancer it's  not even then a guaranteed result that  it would be a cancer we assume that that  kind of thing is too important to ignore  and that was the same spirit in which  these recommendations were made though i  know that there's been a lot of debate  about  how this be implemented and  whether patients should be given more  opportunity to opt out of this and it's  a debate that's likely to continue  although my personal belief is that  these are conditions that  are so overwhelmingly penetrated  medically actionable they'd really be  hard to argue why  that would not report these if they knew  they were present but anyway that can be  discussed at another point  well  to kind 