er splicing patterns  unpredictably or imprinting effects so  that this is a lot more complicated  than it sometimes seems on the surface  so  what are the training needs and i would  argue here that we need to establish  what you could think of as a vector of  competency that begins actually before  matriculation into professional  education for example in undergraduate  years  we need to then  enhance that through their medical  education and for physicians through  residency training and then lifelong  learning through continuing medical  education and maintenance of  certification in the latter may be an  area of opportunity for the already  practicing physician  we also need to do a better job  attracting students to careers in  genetics  from the perspective of both pre-medical  and medical school education  the howard hughes medical institute and  association of american medical colleges  a few years ago issued a report  shown here on scientific foundations for  future physicians but they tried to  identify the competencies in basic  science that would be important for  future physicians and one of their  recommendations was to enhance the  scientific training in the pre-medical  years so that students came to medical  school better prepared and then could  carry that forward through medical  school  this is a very high level document  meaning not a lot of fine detail about  particular curricula this is the one  competency that addresses medical  education in genetics  as the one geneticist on this committee  i guess i can take responsibility for  what was left out of here  but it really is a kind of  all-encompassing thing you might say  this is covering a tremendous amount of  territory and it is and this kind of  competency is paradigmatic of what was  done in other medical specialties too  and it leaves the details of which there  obviously are many to faculty in terms  of actually how to  instill these competencies  in the medical schools and medical  students and training  a few years ago here at uab we actually  extended this concept to not just the  undergraduates who were in pre-health  professional type  programs but also  to general undergraduates thinking you  know maybe you're not going to pursue a  career in health professions but  if you're going to be a consumer of  so-called personalized health care or  genomic medicine you need to be a more  sophisticated consumer  if you're  headed to a career in business law or  engineering there are opportunities we  broke it down into genomics  pharmacogenetics informatics and  cultural and environmental influences on  health and try to identify competencies  that  would be useful in all of these and  customized to these different  usually undergraduate or in some cases  graduate education  kind of modes  so there's a lot to be done in terms of  preparing physicians in general  for the use of genetics and genomics and  for building a professional genetics  workforce also the genetics community  has met twice about this in recent years  at the banbury conference center  near cold spring harbor  once in 2004 and then again in 2006  um the first of those meetings was  focused on how can we increase the  training pipeline into genetics and the  second into defining what the medical  geneticist actually does  since it is a relatively new specialty  that has come of age  originally in the pre-genomic era and  now in the genomic era  the american college of medical genetics  and genomics has defined competencies  for the physician medical geneticist  this particular competency focuses on  translational research and clinical  trials which have become a kind of  mainstream aspect of genetics practice  and this one on providing counseling to  individuals based on genome or exome  sequencing so there's you know a lot  going on there  acmg has also been working to try to  increase the pipeline encouraging  students to  medical students this is  to be aware of career opportunities and  to  be connected  with the genetics programs at your  institution  now for the past three years the summer  scholars program has taken medical  students  one student sometimes two  in 10 to 20 institutions around the  country that apply for a slot  and these students spend the summer with  a stipend provided to acmg foundation  to  actually work in a genetics unit  observing clinics participating in lab  activities sometimes  picking up a project it's been going  only a few years it's probably too soon  to judge how successful it will be but  the hope is that it will provide a kind  of sensitization to the opportunity that  will bring more people and we don't need  every third medical student to go into  genetics but there is the possibility of  doing a genetics residency and certainly  the opportunities to increase the  numbers are really quite significant and  important  we also i think when it comes to  educating the generalist physician or  specialist in other areas you need to  realize not everybody by any means is  going to do a genetics residency which  is a two-year residency by the way  that is recognized by the american board  of medical specialties  but not everybody is going to go that  route or should and that we need to use  every opportunity at our disposal to  educate the workforce through new  educational paradigms i shall hear  examples of the massive open online  courses  khan academy model for  delivering micro lectures on different  topics  we created an ibook here at uab to  support a course we give  on genetics and genomics and clinical  research twice a year to clinical  investigators and other novel paradigms  such as this one  can be used as well  so there'll be lots of opportunities to  try to develop  new approaches  well so what ultimately is needed and i  will identify a few things going forward  um the first is really more of a  scientific challenge but we are a long  way  from having a fully annotated genome  which means that