r and where there have been  active discussions to create those kind  of training paths  and i do think we are going to need new  counseling paradigms when you've got a  genome's worth of information to discuss  with an individual no you can't sit in  the room going gene by gene so i think  we do need to  have new ways of imparting information  probably some of which will be web-based  various apps that  may be able to customize the message to  the particular genetic findings of the  given individual  i think there's huge opportunities and  sort of the entrepreneurial world to be  building both new educational and new  counseling approaches  so i think  in summary i'll make the point that  although  there are many cautions i think i have  alluded to in this talk so far  i'll tell you that i tend to be  optimistic  about how all of this will play out  i  strongly believe that genomics will be a  major engine of progress  in medicine in the coming generation not  the only one but one of the major ones  and for all of the challenges i guess my  fundamental belief is that the medical  community has risen to challenges like  this before  we  cite imaging as an example when i train  mri was just beginning to be introduced  and  now it's it's used on a routine basis  and i think in general well-used i'd be  hard-pressed to tell you any great  detail about the basic physics of mri  but  hasn't stopped me and my colleagues from  using it on a routine basis and i think  um to  better help health of our patients i  think the same will happen in genomics  the  main  way to look at this from my perspective  is this means new  opportunities for those who are inclined  towards sort of creating cutting-edge  paradigms in my mind that's what makes  this time really such an exciting one  which to be practicing  i need to direct you um to this  website which or this link apparently  the actual link is not functioning  but i'll take questions in just a moment  and leave this slide up but those of you  who are  interested in continuing education  credits should know  that you go to this link and  arrange to get your cnes so i will  conclude at this point i very much  appreciate the opportunity to  participate i hope this  was helpful  and  we'll  then  take questions and  notice that  there  are some so i guess one question was  how do we get the pdf that  i publish i think what you're referring  to probably  the pdf of this presentation  will be available on this site but that  may not be what you're referring to i  wonder  if you were referring instead to the  ibook that i  alluded to a few slides back  so that is not in the ibook store it  actually was for a few minutes but we  realized it was probably not didn't  belong there because  these were slides present unprepared for  a particular course that was really very  narrowly targeted  and we're in the process of kind of  upgrading it so that it's  available on a more general basis  anybody specifically interested um can  send me an email which is bcorf uab.edu  and we can talk about how to make that  available if that's  what you're interested in  i don't see any other questions on the  display i guess i can pause for a minute  and see if any  come through the system  okay so the question is there is seeming  disconnect between physicians  and advanced knowledge especially in  basic sciences how can this be bridged  so my answer to that i guess it comes  back to that  hhmi double amc  document and i thought the point of that  first of all  was to rethink the way basic science is  taught and to focus on the things that  really matter in terms of  clinical action because  at least in my mind the goal  is that  you want physicians to be  inquisitive  and to be  interested in and alert to  what the basic science underpinnings of  their practice are  but  you don't necessarily  expect that they are going to be sort of  experts at the basic science  of  every fine detail of everything that  they do